<GuideLayout
  title="Why Use It"
  description="A weak expert is discovered on cross, not before. Gatekeeper front-loads that discovery."
>
  <p>
    A weak expert is usually discovered on cross-examination — a retracted
    paper, a lapsed license, an undisclosed funding source — after the
    engagement is already underway. Gatekeeper front-loads that discovery.
  </p>

  <h2>The cost of finding out late</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Opposing counsel finds the retraction before you do, in front of the jury.</li>
    <li>A license lapse or board sanction surfaces during a <em>Daubert</em> challenge.</li>
    <li>An undisclosed conflict of interest — a funding source, a prior relationship — undermines credibility after testimony is on the record.</li>
    <li>Hours spent manually checking ORCID, PubMed, and state bar sites one name at a time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you get instead</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One score per expert</strong> — LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or CRITICAL — computed from the same signals every time.</li>
    <li><strong>Source-level detail</strong> — see exactly which registry flagged what, not just a pass/fail.</li>
    <li><strong>A record you can hand to co-counsel</strong> — the same dossier, every time, not a patchwork of manual searches.</li>
    <li><strong>Your own instance</strong> — deploy it against your own expert roster; see <a href="/guide/how-to-use/">How To Use</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who it's for</h2>
  <p>
    Litigation teams vetting expert witnesses before retention, MDL/JPML
    coordinators screening a shared expert pool, and firms that want a
    standing, searchable record instead of a one-off Google search before
    each deposition.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
